Link wrote:
> I don't understand how any of this analysis is useful.  People don't
> necessarily choose what they feel is the "best class".  

People choose what they feel they're going to win with.

Some try to pick the "easiest class".  Some then further try to make the
easiest class even easier (startscumming).  But in general, people are
trying to win the game.  (Those who aren't, we've successfully filtered
out the most obvious of -- quit-and-escaped.  There are a lot of
startscummers who deliberately die to an early monster so they don't
show up on the scum list; sadly, there is little I can do to filter
those because they look just like a death by a newbie... even if there
are 100 or more of these for a particular player.  Heck, I watched a
player die with less than 1000 points three times in a row with a Valk
and complain "I can't hit anything!"... when he was taking unnecessary
risks and descending far too fast for his XL.  Well, of course you'll
miss sometimes.)

> For example,
> most of my games are Monks, but that in no way means I think Monks are
> the best class.  However, I've ascended them more often than any other
> class, which by your standards would mean I think Monks are the best
> class.

No.  By my standards, Monks are the best class for _you_.  But the
sample data you describe covers only you, and as such is only valid when
dealing with questions about you.

> I think there are way too many variables in the logfile data, and it
> could never be as good as flat out asking a bunch of people which
> class they feel is the best.

Well, I tried to offer up the "common sense" approach, and someone
objected.  So I hauled out a big-ass sample set and beat on it with a
hammer for awhile, and the results came out very similar to "common
sense" -- except that Caveman and Knight are likely misrepresented in
the list because the avoidance of those roles by average players is _so_
large that there just isn't enough data to properly compare.

> Also, I think someone mentioned earlier that quest artifacts affected
> which class is the best.  I disagree with that too, because by the
> time you can get your quest artifact, you've already done all the hard
> parts of the game.  

While Gehennom is nothing short of boring on the way down and the Castle
can be trivial if properly prepared for, the trip back up is not
necessarily a piece of cake, and the Planes present their own
challenges.  Some quest artifacts make this process noticeably easier
than others.

The quest artifact is far from being the sole determinant, but an easy
class that gets a strong quest artifact starts to become VERY easy to
finish out.  (Orb of Weight, PYEC, and Eye of Broken, we're talkin' to
you here.)

There are other strong artifacts (Staff and Eyes come to mind), but in
the first instance the early-game Healer is far from easy, and while the
Monk isn't weak, there are some very specific problems he must deal with
in order to get very far.

> I still think the hardest part of the game is
> retrieving the luckstone, and surviving the trip back to the main
> dungeon.  The luckstone is such a game breaking item, that many of the
> classes are on easy-mode once the luckstone is maxed out.

I'm going to assume you're just being humorous here, because the
luckstone is nowhere near good enough to guarantee ascension by itself.
 In the hands of a skilled player who knows how to best maximize its
advantages, the luckstone is well worth obtaining... but for the average
player, there's still a lot to do that a luckstone won't help with.

